(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-cms-exam-2-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 61-year-old woman is being assessed for glaucoma. What can the intraocular pressure be?","choices":["Always below normal","Always markedly elevated","Either normal or elevated","Elevated only in the morning","Irrelevant, as pressure is never measured"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — pressure may be normal OR raised in this disease. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 18"},{"q":"What proportion of anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy is the non-arteritic form?","choices":["About half","Ninety to ninety-five per cent","Under five per cent","About twenty-five per cent","About ten per cent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is by far the commoner form. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the term for lashes turned in against the globe?","choices":["Anisocoria","Proptosis","Chemosis","Trichiasis","Leukocoria"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the consequence of entropion that threatens the cornea. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man woke with a painful lump at the margin of his right lower lid. It was not there yesterday. Examination shows a tender subcutaneous nodule at the lid margin with surrounding erythema. Vision and pupils are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pre-septal cellulitis","Hordeolum","Dermatochalasis","Xanthelasma","Chalazion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute, usually staphylococcal, and the two features that separate it from a chalazion are that it is TENDER and that it appeared within 24 hours. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has dacryoadenitis. Which statement about imaging is correct?","choices":["Contrast computed tomography when indicated","Imaging is never indicated","Every patient needs imaging on presentation","Imaging is required before antibiotics","Only magnetic resonance imaging is acceptable"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — orbits and sinuses, when there is concern about orbital involvement. Orbits and sinuses, not automatically on presentation. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which lens corrects hyperopia, and how?","choices":["A convex lens, converging light forward","A toric lens","A concave lens, which scatters light","A prism","A tinted lens"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — positive dioptre. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has recurrent episodes of sectoral eye redness with mild irritation, each resolving within a week or two. Vision is always normal. What is the appropriate management?","choices":["Topical antibiotics","Long-term oral corticosteroids","Urgent systemic workup for vasculitis","Reassurance, with lubricants for comfort","Immediate ophthalmology referral"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Episcleritis is self-limiting and often needs no specific treatment. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 48"},{"q":"How is uveal melanoma usually discovered?","choices":["Through recurrent conjunctivitis","Through sudden painful vision loss","Incidentally","Through a red painful eye","Through bilateral field loss"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is often found on routine examination. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 67-year-old hypertensive man has sudden painless blurring of the right eye. Fundoscopy shows widespread retinal haemorrhages in all four quadrants with dilated tortuous veins and disc swelling. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Papilledema","Branch retinal vein occlusion","Central retinal vein occlusion","Diabetic retinopathy","Central retinal artery occlusion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The 'blood and thunder' fundus — haemorrhages in every quadrant with engorged veins. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man has a corneal ulcer. Which examination finding indicates significant intraocular inflammation?","choices":["Ciliary flush alone","A hyphema","A cherry-red spot","A hypopyon","Drusen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — layered white cells in the anterior chamber. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man who was striking metal has vertical linear scratches on the cornea. What do they indicate?","choices":["A chemical burn","Acute angle closure","A dendritic herpetic ulcer","A bacterial ulcer","A foreign body under the upper lid"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — linear vertical epithelial defects point to a retained particle sweeping the cornea with each blink. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which type of retinal detachment is NOT primarily treated surgically?","choices":["Rhegmatogenous","All three are surgical","Traction","Combined rhegmatogenous and traction","Exudative"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. There is no break and no traction, so the underlying disease is treated. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has a red, painful swelling over the inner corner of his left lower lid. Pressure over the swelling expresses pus from the lower punctum. He is afebrile and systemically well. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Topical antibiotic drops alone","Immediate surgical excision of the sac","Oral antibiotics for 10 days","Observation with warm compresses only","Admit for intravenous antibiotics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A well, afebrile, reliable patient is managed as an outpatient on orals. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 25"},{"q":"Why is the ptosis of Horner syndrome mild?","choices":["The lid is swollen rather than drooping","The third nerve is intact but weak","The eye is proptosed, masking it","Levator function is normal","It is not mild; it is complete"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the sympathetically supplied Muller muscle is the one affected. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has a painful, photophobic red right eye with reduced vision. Fluorescein shows diffuse punctate uptake across the cornea without a discrete infiltrate. She has been using a hairdryer at close range and has dry eyes. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Herpes simplex keratitis","Punctate epithelial keratitis","Anterior uveitis","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Bacterial corneal ulcer"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Diffuse punctate staining without an infiltrate, from exposure and surface drying. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which procedure is named for draining blood from within the orbit?","choices":["Vitrectomy","Scleral buckle","Peripheral iridotomy","Canthotomy with cantholysis","Trabeculoplasty"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — releasing the lateral canthal tendon and cutting its inferior branch. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has sudden ptosis of the right lid. The right eye is deviated DOWN and OUT, and the right pupil is dilated and unreactive. He has a severe headache. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Check blood glucose and observe","Start topical antiglaucoma drops","Prescribe an eye patch and analgesia","Urgent imaging for a posterior communicating aneurysm","Reassure and review in one month"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A pupil-INVOLVING third nerve palsy with headache is an aneurysm until proven otherwise. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man finds he must hold text further away to read it, while distance vision is unchanged. What is the mechanism?","choices":["The eye becomes too short","The lens stiffens, so accommodation fails","The cornea becomes irregular","The eye becomes too long","The lens becomes opaque"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the A in PERRLA is what is failing. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with optic neuritis asks what it might mean for her long term. Which association should be discussed?","choices":["Hypertension","Giant cell arteritis","Diabetes mellitus","Thyroid eye disease","Multiple sclerosis"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. It is a common presenting feature, and imaging looks for demyelinating lesions. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has confirmed acute angle-closure glaucoma with an intraocular pressure of 58 mm Hg. What is the definitive treatment once the pressure is controlled?","choices":["Observation once the pressure falls","Cataract extraction as first line","Topical pilocarpine indefinitely","Laser peripheral iridotomy","Long-term topical beta blocker alone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it creates an alternative route for aqueous and prevents recurrence. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 17"},{"q":"In wet macular degeneration, what does the choroidal neovascularisation eventually form?","choices":["Drusen","A macular hole","An epiretinal membrane","A cherry-red spot","A disciform scar"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — it bleeds into the retina and organises. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man noticed a bright red patch on the white of his right eye this morning after a bout of coughing. There is no pain, vision is normal, the pupil is reactive and the cornea is clear. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Check the blood pressure","Immediate haematology referral","Urgent ophthalmology referral","Computed tomography of the orbits","Start topical antibiotics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With no explanation for the bleed, blood pressure is checked; otherwise the history is the workup. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman with headaches worse on waking has transient visual obscurations lasting seconds. Fundoscopy shows BILATERAL swollen optic discs. Visual acuity is 6/6 in both eyes. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Refer routinely to optometry","Start high-dose corticosteroids for arteritis","Neuroimaging, then lumbar puncture","Reassure, since acuity is normal","Start topical antiglaucoma drops"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bilateral disc swelling means raised intracranial pressure until proven otherwise. If imaging is normal. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the commonest cause of a sixth nerve palsy in CHILDREN?","choices":["Microvascular disease","Trauma","Multiple sclerosis","Intracranial tumour","Congenital malformation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Especially brainstem and posterior fossa. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 9-day-old infant has bilateral lid swelling with a watery-to-mucopurulent discharge. Gonococcal disease has been excluded. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Topical erythromycin ointment alone","Oral erythromycin","A single dose of intramuscular ceftriaxone","Observation, as neonatal conjunctivitis is self-limiting","Topical acyclovir"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Systemic treatment is needed because chlamydia also colonises the nasopharynx and risks pneumonitis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has a vesicular rash over her right forehead and the tip of her nose, with a red painful eye. Which cranial nerve division is involved?","choices":["The optic nerve","The maxillary division of the trigeminal nerve","The ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve","The facial nerve","The oculomotor nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — V1 of cranial nerve V. Involvement of the nose tip signals ocular involvement. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has a painful vesicular rash in a band across his right forehead, sparing the midline, with vesicles on the tip of his nose. What does the nasal tip involvement indicate?","choices":["That the cause is herpes simplex rather than zoster","A high likelihood of ocular involvement","That the rash will cross the midline","That the trigeminal ganglion is spared","That antiviral treatment is unnecessary"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — Hutchinson sign; the nasociliary branch supplies both the nose tip and the globe. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which structures does POSTERIOR uveitis involve?","choices":["The episclera","The iris alone","The iris and ciliary body","The lacrimal gland","The choroid and/or the retina"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — choroiditis and retinitis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 64"},{"q":"A neonate is found to have a dense unilateral cataract. How does the timing of surgery differ from an adult?","choices":["Early, to prevent amblyopia","It is never performed in neonates","It is deferred until school age","It is deferred until the child can cooperate","It is done only if both eyes are affected"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — in adults surgery waits until function is affected. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with long-standing type 2 diabetes has increasing glare, particularly when driving at night. Slit lamp examination shows spoke-like opacities radiating from the periphery of the lens. Which cataract type is this?","choices":["Congenital","Cortical","Traumatic","Nuclear","Posterior subcapsular"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the spoke-like peripheral wedges, classically associated with diabetes. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 37"},{"q":"When is the anisocoria of Horner syndrome most evident?","choices":["In bright light","Only after pharmacological testing","In the first four to five seconds","It is equal in all conditions","Only on near effort"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the dilation lag. After the lights are dimmed. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman with papilledema is investigated. In what order?","choices":["Lumbar puncture first, then imaging","Carotid Doppler","Tonometry alone","Temporal artery biopsy","Imaging first, then lumbar puncture"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — a raised opening pressure confirms it. Imaging excludes a mass. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman with suspected giant cell arteritis has vision loss in one eye. What investigation confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["Visual field testing","Temporal artery biopsy","Carotid Doppler","Fluorescein angiography alone","Optical coherence tomography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — but treatment starts before the result. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has had three subconjunctival haemorrhages in six months. She takes no anticoagulants. What is the appropriate approach?","choices":["Start topical corticosteroids","Urgent ophthalmology referral","Medication and blood pressure review","Automatic referral to haematology","Reassurance with no investigation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — targeted rather than an automatic haematology referral. With a targeted haematologic evaluation. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman has had cataract surgery. Which later complication is she now more likely to develop?","choices":["Posterior vitreous detachment","Trachoma","Amblyopia","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Optic neuritis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — cataract surgery, myopia and ocular trauma all make it more likely. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 45-year-old contact lens wearer has bacterial conjunctivitis that has not improved in 24 hours. What is the appropriate disposition?","choices":["Discharge with reassurance","Stop all treatment and observe","Urgent referral","Switch to oral antibiotics and review in a month","Routine review in two weeks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — lens wear plus failure to improve in 24 hours both make it urgent. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man has pupils of unequal size. The difference is GREATER in bright light. Which pupil is abnormal?","choices":["Neither; this is physiological","Both are abnormal","The smaller pupil, which is failing to dilate","It cannot be determined without imaging","The larger pupil, which is failing to constrict"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — a defect of constriction shows up in the light. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man has non-arteritic ischaemic optic neuropathy. Which structural feature is associated with it?","choices":["A small optic disc, the 'disc at risk'","A thin cornea","A large excavated optic cup","A shallow anterior chamber","A long axial length"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside hypertension, diabetes, high cholesterol and sleep apnoea. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 52"},{"q":"How is nystagmus named?","choices":["For its frequency","For the direction of the FAST beat","For the underlying cause","For the eye that is affected","For the direction of the slow beat"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man has a traumatic cranial nerve palsy causing diplopia. What is the management?","choices":["High-dose corticosteroids","Immediate corrective surgery","Topical pressure-lowering drops","Observe for six months, patching one eye","Urgent neuroimaging in every case"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the same approach for third, fourth and sixth nerve trauma. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which lid laceration features require ophthalmology referral?","choices":["Margin involvement, or near the medial canthus","Only lacerations in children","Only bleeding lacerations","Any laceration of the eyelid skin","Only lacerations longer than 3 cm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, along with lacrimal duct or sac involvement and inner lid surface injury. Within 6 to 8 mm of it. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the goal of hyphema management?","choices":["Preventing a rebleed","Lowering the risk of retinal detachment","Preventing infection","Absorbing the blood as fast as possible","Restoring vision immediately"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — bed rest with the head elevated, antiemetics, ocular hypotensives, corticosteroids and a cycloplegic. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man with viral conjunctivitis says his eye looked worse on day five than on day two and asks whether the treatment has failed. What should he be told?","choices":["It worsens for a week, then clears","He should stop the artificial tears","Worsening means a bacterial superinfection has developed","It should have resolved within 48 hours","He needs topical antibiotics now"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is highly contagious throughout. Resolution takes two to three weeks. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy has headaches and eye strain when reading, and his distance vision is normal. Which refractive error is most likely?","choices":["Presbyopia","Astigmatism","Hyperopia","Myopia","Amblyopia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the eye is too short and accommodative effort at near produces the strain. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which is the commonest paediatric cataract type?","choices":["Posterior subcapsular","Cortical","Nuclear","Polar","Zonular"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — a white opacity around the nucleus. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which structure is inflamed in keratitis?","choices":["The choroid","The sclera","The iris","The episcleral tissue","The cornea"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which red eye presentations are seen the SAME DAY rather than immediately?","choices":["Chemical injury, open globe and angle closure","Keratitis, uveitis, scleritis, zoster","Blepharitis and dry eye","Subconjunctival haemorrhage","Pinguecula and pterygium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — emergent means now; these four are same day. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 32-year-old teacher with viral conjunctivitis asks what she should do about work. What is the key advice?","choices":["Antibiotic drops will make her non-infectious","She may share towels if they are washed weekly","No precautions are needed once tears stop","Strict hand hygiene, as it is highly contagious","She should avoid reading to rest the eye"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — contagious precautions are the central education point. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man was punched in the right eye. He has double vision when looking UP, numbness of the right cheek and upper lip, and the eye appears sunken. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Medial wall fracture","Orbital contusion","Traumatic optic neuropathy","Globe rupture","Orbital floor (blowout) fracture"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Inferior rectus entrapment limits upgaze and the infraorbital nerve supplies the cheek. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman has a raised pigmented conjunctival lesion that has enlarged over six months and has its own feeding vessels. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure, as conjunctival pigment is common in this age group","Photograph and review in two years","Urgent referral for biopsy","Prescribe lubricating drops","Start topical antibiotics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Growth and intrinsic vascularity are the concerning features. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman describes transient monocular vision loss lasting minutes. What is the underlying mechanism?","choices":["Acute rise in intraocular pressure","Retinal break with subretinal fluid","Vitreous haemorrhage","Transient retinal ischaemia, usually embolic","Optic nerve demyelination"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — most often from carotid atheroma. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is the initial treatment of idiopathic intracranial hypertension?","choices":["Acetazolamide and weight reduction","High-dose corticosteroids","Topical pressure-lowering drops","Immediate optic nerve sheath fenestration","Observation alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — surgical cerebrospinal fluid diversion only if medical therapy fails. Supervised weight reduction. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has acquired nystagmus with oscillopsia. What does the acquired onset suggest?","choices":["An underlying neurological cause","A benign congenital variant","Anxiety alone","A refractive error","Normal ageing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — acquired nystagmus in an adult is not benign. It requires investigation. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man with a hyphema is given instructions for the next five days. Which activity must he avoid?","choices":["Sleeping on his back","Reading","Drinking fluids","Strenuous activity and bending","Watching television"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — both raise the risk of rebleeding. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 25"},{"q":"A patient presents with a red eye. Which two findings are the red flags that separate a dangerous cause from a benign one?","choices":["Reduced vision and true pain","Discharge and lid crusting","Itching and watering","Bilateral involvement and recent contact","Foreign body sensation and tearing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — those two are what move a red eye out of the benign category. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man with posterior uveitis is being investigated for an infectious cause. Which organism causes chorioretinitis?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus","Chlamydia trachomatis","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Toxoplasma gondii","Adenovirus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a classic infectious cause of posterior uveitis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has a rhegmatogenous retinal detachment. Which immediate measures are named alongside the referral?","choices":["Immediate irrigation of the eye","Topical antibiotics","Firm padding of the eye","Lying flat with the head lowered","Pain control, antiemetics, head of bed up"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — ophthalmology STAT with those supportive measures. Thirty to forty degrees. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with hypertension and sleep apnoea wakes with painless loss of the lower half of the visual field in one eye. There is no scalp tenderness or jaw claudication, and the inflammatory markers are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Retinal detachment","Non-arteritic anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy","Optic neuritis","Central retinal artery occlusion","Arteritic anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Altitudinal field loss on waking, with normal inflammatory markers and no arteritic symptoms. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 51-year-old woman has bumped into objects on both sides for months. Formal fields show loss of BOTH temporal hemifields. Where is the lesion?","choices":["In one optic nerve","In the retina of one eye","In the optic tract","In the occipital cortex","At the optic chiasm"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Bitemporal (heteronymous) hemianopsia is the chiasmal pattern on the visual pathway diagram. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 34-year-old contact lens wearer has a red right eye with thick yellow discharge and lids stuck together on waking. There is no preauricular node. What makes this presentation urgent rather than routine?","choices":["The absence of a preauricular node","Contact lens wear","The unilateral involvement","The lids being stuck together","The thick discharge"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Contact lens wear, immunocompromise, recent surgery, a foreign body, corneal opacity or no improvement in 24 hours all make it urgent. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 41"},{"q":"Why is famotidine added when high-dose corticosteroids are started for giant cell arteritis?","choices":["To lower intraocular pressure","To potentiate the steroid","Ulcer prophylaxis","To prevent osteoporosis","To treat the headache"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has a black eye after being punched. Which finding would make this more than a simple contusion?","choices":["Bruising extending to the cheek","A relative afferent pupillary defect","Tenderness over the orbital rim","Lid swelling that limits opening","Subconjunctival haemorrhage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — that indicates optic nerve involvement and changes the urgency entirely. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 33"},{"q":"Why is a third nerve palsy described as producing an eye that is 'down and out'?","choices":["Gravity pulls the globe down","The inferior oblique overacts","The medial rectus overacts","The levator contracts abnormally","Unopposed lateral rectus and superior oblique"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — the muscles the third nerve does NOT supply are the ones left working. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with a raised body mass index has daily headaches, pulsatile tinnitus and transient visual obscurations. Fundoscopy shows bilateral disc swelling. Neuroimaging is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Malignant hypertension","Migraine with aura","Idiopathic intracranial hypertension","Giant cell arteritis","Optic neuritis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the classic demographic, with papilledema and normal imaging. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 4"},{"q":"How is an iris nevus followed up?","choices":["More often at first, then annually","Immediate excision","No follow-up at all","A single review and then discharge","Monthly review indefinitely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — surveillance with dilated review. The early checks confirm it is not growing. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 49"}]};
